---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ScreenRecorder from '@/components/tools/media/ScreenRecorder.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="screen-recorder">
  <ScreenRecorder client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to record your screen</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide whether you want your microphone and the computer’s own audio, then press <strong>Start recording</strong>.</li>
      <li>Your browser shows a picker with your screens, open windows and tabs. Choose one. The page cannot see or change this picker: only you decide what is shared.</li>
      <li>Switch to what you want to record. This tab keeps capturing in the background, and the length and file size counters keep running.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Stop and save</strong> here, or the browser’s own "Stop sharing" bar. Preview the result, download it, and optionally convert it to MP4.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where the video goes</h2>
    <p>
      Frames go from the browser’s capture API into the MediaRecorder API and into memory in this tab. When
      you press stop, they become a file in your downloads folder. There is no upload step, processing
      server or storage bucket, and this site’s content security policy lists no third-party origin that a
      request could go to.
    </p>
    <p>
      A screen recording contains everything that was on your screen: an email client, a bank tab, an
      internal dashboard, customer data, a password manager you forgot was open. To check that nothing is
      sent, open the Network panel in your developer tools and record for a minute, or disconnect your
      network; the screen recorder keeps working.
    </p>

    <h2>Audio capture by browser</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Browser</th>
            <th>Microphone</th>
            <th>Tab audio</th>
            <th>Whole-system audio</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Chrome / Edge on Windows</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>Yes: tick "Share tab audio"</td>
            <td>Yes: tick "Share system audio"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Chrome / Edge on macOS</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>Yes: tick "Share tab audio"</td>
            <td>No (an operating-system restriction)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Firefox</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>No</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Safari</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>No</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mobile browsers</td>
            <td colspan="3">Screen capture is not available; use your phone’s built-in recorder</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When a microphone and a system source are both available, the Web Audio API mixes them into one track
      before recording. The result is one file with your commentary over the audio from your screen. The
      on-page preview stays muted, because playing your own system audio through the speakers would cause a
      feedback loop.
    </p>

    <h2>Recording length and memory</h2>
    <p>
      There is no watermark, time limit or resolution cap. The limit is memory: the tab holds the recording
      until you save it. At the balanced quality preset, allow about 30 MB per minute, and save very long
      sessions in parts.
    </p>

    <h2>WebM, MP4, and the missing duration</h2>
    <p>
      Browsers record WebM natively. MediaRecorder does not write the duration into the WebM header, so many
      players show no timeline and cannot seek. Recent Chrome versions can record H.264 in MP4 directly, and
      the setup panel shows that option when it is available. If not, the convert button re-encodes the WebM
      to MP4 with FFmpeg on this page, which also fixes the header. If your editor accepts WebM, skip that
      step.
    </p>

    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Nothing happens when you press start: the picker was closed or screen capture is blocked. On macOS, allow your browser under System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Screen Recording, then restart the browser.</li>
      <li>The recording is silent: the audio checkbox in the picker was not ticked, or your browser cannot capture system audio (see the table above).</li>
      <li>The recording stopped early: closing the shared window or tab ends the capture. The tool keeps what it captured before that point.</li>
      <li>The file is very large: choose the small quality preset. For a screen recording it usually looks the same, because most of the frame does not change.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>After recording</h2>
    <p>
      Recordings often need more work: <a href="/trim-video">trim</a> the silence at the start,
      <a href="/crop-video">crop</a> to the important region, <a href="/compress-video">compress</a> to fit
      an upload limit, or <a href="/mp4-to-mp3">extract the audio</a> for a transcript. These tools also run
      in the browser, so the recording stays on your computer.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
